## Is Raynaud's a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. Mild Raynaud's may not meet this. Severe or secondary Raynaud's, or the condition causing it, often will.

## Reasonable adjustments

Changes that can help at work include:

- a **warmer** place to work, away from draughts and air conditioning
- a desk **heater**, or permission to wear gloves and extra layers
- avoiding work in **cold stores**, freezers or outdoors in cold weather
- regular breaks to warm up
- tools with warm or padded handles
- time off for appointments

## Hand-arm vibration syndrome

Regularly using **vibrating tools**, such as drills, grinders, chainsaws or hammers, can damage the nerves and blood vessels in your hands. This can cause Raynaud's symptoms, sometimes called **vibration white finger**.

- employers must **control the risk** from vibration under health and safety law, and provide **health checks** for workers at risk
- tell your employer and GP if you get symptoms, as stopping or reducing exposure early can stop it getting worse
- you may be able to claim **Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit**, because it is a recognised work-related disease

## Benefits

**PIP** is based on how your condition affects you on most days, not on the diagnosis. If Raynaud's or a related condition makes it hard to use your hands, think about activities such as:

- **preparing food**, for example peeling, chopping or holding hot or cold things
- **washing and bathing**, and **dressing**, especially fastenings
- **managing treatments**, such as applying creams

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
